window.UNI_PULSE_SOURCE_RECORDS = [{"subjectId":"pea101","title":"Problem Understanding and Representation","sourceUrl":"resources/PSCT-Unit-1-source-text.txt","sourceFormat":"text","topics":[{"id":"framing","title":"Problem Identification and Framing","text":"<PARSED TEXT FOR PAGE: 1 / 32>\nUNIT I – Problem Understanding and Representation\n\n   1. Problem Identification and Framing\n   2. Requirements and Expected Outcomes\n   3. Constraints and Assumptions\n   4. Problem Decomposition\n   5. Abstraction and Relevant Information\n   6. Structured Problem Representation\n\n\n\nThis unit explains how to understand a problem clearly, identify what needs to be\nsolved, determine requirements and limitations, divide a complex problem into\nsmaller parts, identify relevant information, and represent the problem in a\nstructured way.\n\n\nLearning objective:\nBy the end of this unit, students should be able to understand a problem before\nattempting to solve it, especially when developing algorithms, programs, or\nengineering solutions.\n\n\n1. Problem Identification and Framing\n1.1 What is a problem?\nA problem is a situation in which we want to achieve a goal, but the method to\nachieve that goal is not immediately clear.\nSimple examples\n      A student wants to improve examination marks but does not know how to\n       plan their study.\n      A college wants to reduce canteen waiting time.\n      A program needs to find the largest number in an array.\n      A farmer wants to reduce water usage in agriculture.\nIn each case, there is a current situation and a desired outcome.\n\n\n1.2 What is problem identification?\nProblem identification is the process of recognizing and understanding what is\nwrong, missing, inefficient, or needs improvement.\n\n                                        1\n<PARSED TEXT FOR PAGE: 2 / 32>\nExample: College Canteen\nCurrent situation:\n      Students wait a long time to purchase food.\n      Several students arrive during the lunch break.\n      The available counters may not serve everyone quickly.\nIdentified problem:\n\"Students experience long waiting times in the college canteen during lunch\nhours.\"\nNotice that the problem is stated clearly without immediately assuming that\nadding more counters is the only solution.\n\n\n1.3 What is problem framing?\nProblem framing means expressing the identified problem in a clear and focused\nway so that the correct problem is addressed.\nA poorly framed problem can lead to an ineffective solution.\nPoor problem framing\n\"The canteen needs more counters.\"\nThis assumes a solution before investigating the cause.\nBetter problem framing\n\"Students experience long waiting times during lunch because the ordering and\nserving process may not handle peak demand efficiently.\"\nThis identifies the problem and leaves room to investigate its causes.\n\n\n1.4 Elements of a good problem statement\nA good problem statement should explain:\n   1. Current situation: What is happening now?\n   2. Problem: What is wrong or needs improvement?\n   3. Affected people or systems: Who experiences the problem?\n   4. Desired outcome: What should change?\n   5. Scope: Which part of the problem will be addressed?\n\n\n\n\n                                        2\n<PARSED TEXT FOR PAGE: 3 / 32>\nExample: Student Marks\nProblem statement:\n\"Students in a class are scoring below the expected level in mathematics. The\nobjective is to identify the main learning difficulties and develop a study-support\napproach to improve their understanding.\"\nThis statement explains the current situation, problem, and objective without\nassuming a single cause.\n\n\n1.5 Difference between a problem and a solution\nProblem                                         Possible solution\nStudents wait too long in the canteen           Online ordering or improved service\nStudents cannot find books easily               Library search system\nElectricity is wasted in empty classrooms Automatic lighting controls\nStudents struggle to track assignments          Assignment              management\n                                                application\nImportant rule: First understand the problem; then identify possible solutions.\n\n\n1.6 Steps for problem identification and framing\n   1. Observe the situation.\n   2. Identify the gap between the current and desired situations.\n   3. Collect relevant information.\n   4. Identify who is affected.\n   5. Investigate possible causes.\n   6. Write a clear problem statement.\n   7. Confirm that the statement addresses the actual problem.\nProgramming example\nProblem: Find the largest number in an array.\n      Current situation: An array contains several numbers.\n      Goal: Identify the largest number.\n      Input: An array of numbers.\n      Output: The maximum value.\n      Success condition: The output must be greater than or equal to every array\n       element.\n                                            3\n<PARSED TEXT FOR PAGE: 4 / 32>\nThe problem is now clear enough to develop an algorithm.\n\n\n"},{"id":"requirements","title":"Requirements and Expected Outcomes","text":"2. Requirements and Expected Outcomes\n2.1 What are requirements?\nRequirements describe what a solution must do, provide, or satisfy.\nThey help us understand what is necessary before designing a solution.\nExample: Online Library System\nPossible requirements:\n      Students must be able to search for books.\n      Students must be able to check book availability.\n      Librarians must be able to add and update books.\n      The system must protect user information.\nIf requirements are incomplete, the final system may not satisfy users' needs.\n\n\n2.2 Types of requirements\nA. Functional requirements\nFunctional requirements describe what a system should do.\nExamples:\n      Allow a student to log in.\n      Search for a book.\n      Calculate student attendance percentage.\n      Generate examination results.\n      Display the largest number in an array.\nB. Non-functional requirements\nNon-functional requirements describe how well a system should work or the\nconditions under which it must operate.\nExamples:\n      The system should respond quickly.\n      User information must be protected.\n      The application should be easy to use.\n      The system should work reliably.\n      The application should support many users.\n\n\n                                          4\n<PARSED TEXT FOR PAGE: 5 / 32>\nComparison\nFunctional requirement Non-functional requirement\nWhat the system does       How well the system works\nCalculate attendance       Calculate it accurately and efficiently\nSearch for a book          Return results quickly\nLog in to the system       Protect login credentials\n\n\n2.3 What are expected outcomes?\nAn expected outcome is the result that should be achieved after solving the\nproblem.\nA good expected outcome should be clear and, when possible, measurable.\nExample: Reducing Canteen Waiting Time\nExpected outcomes might include:\n      Reduce average waiting time.\n      Reduce ordering errors.\n      Improve student satisfaction.\n      Maintain affordable operating costs.\nSuppose the current average waiting time is 12 minutes and the target is 6\nminutes.\nThe target is measurable: reduce average waiting time by 50%.\nThese are illustrative targets; actual targets should be based on collected data.\n\n\n2.4 Inputs, processing, and outputs\nMany computational problems can be represented using the following structure:\n\n\n\n\n                                         5\n<PARSED TEXT FOR PAGE: 6 / 32>\nExample: Calculate the average of three marks\n      Input: 70, 80, 90\n      Processing: Add the marks and divide by 3.\n      Output: 80\nExpected outcome: The program should calculate and display the correct average.\n\n\n2.5 How do we check whether requirements are satisfied?\nWe define success criteria.\nExamples:\n      The program returns the correct answer.\n      The application meets the required response time.\n      The system prevents unauthorized access.\n      The solution stays within the budget.\n      Users can complete the intended task successfully.\nKey idea: Requirements describe what is needed; expected outcomes describe\nwhat we want to achieve; success criteria explain how we will verify achievement.\n\n\n"},{"id":"constraints","title":"Constraints and Assumptions","text":"3. Constraints and Assumptions\n3.1 What are constraints?\nA constraint is a limitation or restriction that a solution must satisfy.\nConstraints affect which solutions are possible or acceptable.\n\n\n\n\n                                          6\n<PARSED TEXT FOR PAGE: 7 / 32>\nCommon types of constraints\n Constraint             Meaning                               Example\nTime           Limited time available           Finish the project in 2 weeks\nBudget         Limited money                    Develop an app within ₹10,000\n               Limited storage or\nMemory                                          Program must use limited RAM\n               memory\nHardware       Available equipment              Only 10 computers are available\nTechnology Technical limitations                Internet access is unavailable\n                                                Only authorized users can access\nSecurity       Required protection\n                                                records\n               Limited people or\nResources                                       Only 3 students are available\n               materials\n\n\nExample: College Event Application\nA team wants to develop an event registration application.\nConstraints:\n      Development must finish within 15 days.\n      Only three students are available.\n      The budget is limited.\n      The application must protect student information.\nA solution requiring expensive hardware and several months of development may\nnot be appropriate.\n\n\n3.2 What are assumptions?\nAn assumption is something we accept as true for the purpose of analysing or\nsolving a problem, even though it may not have been fully verified.\nAssumptions help us proceed when complete information is unavailable.\nExample: College Attendance\nSuppose we are calculating attendance percentage.\nWe may assume:\n      All classes have equal weight.\n      The attendance records are accurate.\n\n\n                                            7\n<PARSED TEXT FOR PAGE: 8 / 32>\n      The total number of classes is known.\nThese assumptions simplify the calculation. However, if some classes have\ndifferent rules or the records are incorrect, the result may not reflect the actual\nattendance.\n\n\n3.3 Difference between constraints and assumptions\nConstraints                            Assumptions\nLimit what the solution can do         State what we temporarily accept as true\nUsually imposed by the problem or Used to simplify analysis or handle\nenvironment                            missing information\nExample: Budget is ₹10,000             Example: Internet is available\nMust be respected or addressed         Should be checked when possible\n\n\n3.4 Boundary conditions\nA boundary condition describes a special case at the edge of the allowed input or\noperating range.\nBoundary conditions are important because solutions that work for normal cases\nmay fail at the limits.\nExample 1: Voting Eligibility\nSuppose the rule is that a person can vote at age 18 or above.\n      Age 17: Not eligible.\n      Age 18: Eligible.\n      Age 19: Eligible.\nThe age of exactly 18 is an important boundary case.\nExample 2: Array\nSuppose an array contains N elements.\n      Empty array: N=0\n      One element: N=1\n      Multiple elements: N>1\nAn algorithm that finds the largest element must handle an empty array\nappropriately rather than assuming an element exists.\n\n\n\n\n                                         8\n<PARSED TEXT FOR PAGE: 9 / 32>\n3.5 Why must we identify constraints and assumptions?\nThey help us:\n   1. Avoid unrealistic solutions.\n   2. Estimate required time and resources.\n   3. Identify missing information.\n   4. Make decisions logically.\n   5. Handle special cases.\n   6. Reduce unexpected failures.\nPractice example\nA college wants to build a student-result portal.\nIdentify:\n      Two constraints\n      Two assumptions\n      Two boundary conditions\nPossible considerations include development time, budget, availability of student\nrecords, zero students in a class, and a student with marks at the minimum or\nmaximum allowed value.\n\n\n"},{"id":"decomposition","title":"Problem Decomposition","text":"4. Problem Decomposition\n4.1 What is problem decomposition?\nProblem decomposition is the process of breaking a large or complex problem into\nsmaller, manageable subproblems.\nA complex problem may be difficult to understand and solve as one large task.\nDividing it into smaller tasks makes the work easier to plan, implement, test, and\nmaintain.\nExample: College Management System\nDeveloping an entire college management system is a large task.\nWe can divide it into:\n      Student registration\n      Faculty management\n      Attendance management\n      Examination management\n      Fee management\n\n                                         9\n<PARSED TEXT FOR PAGE: 10 / 32>\n      Timetable management\n      Report generation\nEach module can be designed and tested separately.\n\n\n4.2 Why is decomposition useful?\n1. Reduces complexity\nSmaller tasks are easier to understand.\n2. Improves planning\nWe can estimate the time and resources needed for each subproblem.\n3. Supports teamwork\nDifferent team members can work on different modules.\n4. Makes testing easier\nEach component can be tested independently.\n5. Simplifies debugging\nErrors can be located within a smaller part of the system.\n6. Encourages reuse\nA useful component may be reused in another project.\n\n\n4.3 Decomposition example: Student Result System\n\n\n\n\nEach subproblem has a specific responsibility.\nFor example, the validation module checks whether marks are within the\npermitted range before the calculation module processes them.\n\n\n\n\n                                          10\n<PARSED TEXT FOR PAGE: 11 / 32>\n4.4 Decomposition in programming\nConsider the problem of calculating the average of five subjects and determining\nthe result.\nWe can divide it into:\n   1. Read five marks.\n   2. Validate the marks.\n   3. Calculate the total.\n   4. Calculate the average.\n   5. Determine the result or grade according to the given rules.\n   6. Display the result.\nEach step can be developed as a separate function if appropriate.\n\n\n4.5 Top-down approach\nThe top-down approach starts with the complete problem and progressively\nbreaks it into smaller subproblems.\nExample:\nStudent Result System\n  |\n  |-- Read Marks\n  |-- Validate Marks\n  |-- Calculate Total\n  |-- Calculate Average\n  |-- Determine Grade\n  |-- Display Result\nWe start with the overall goal and move toward smaller tasks.\n\n\n4.6 Important point: Decomposition and dependencies\nSometimes one subproblem depends on another.\nFor example:\n      Marks must be entered before they can be validated.\n      Marks must be validated before calculating the total.\n      The total must be calculated before calculating the average.\n\n\n                                        11\n<PARSED TEXT FOR PAGE: 12 / 32>\nTherefore, decomposition should identify not only the smaller tasks but also their\norder and dependencies.\n\n\n"},{"id":"abstraction","title":"Abstraction and Relevant Information","text":"5. Abstraction and Relevant Information\n5.1 What is abstraction?\nAbstraction means focusing on the important details of a problem while ignoring\ndetails that are not necessary for the current task.\nIt helps reduce complexity by concentrating on what matters.\nSimple everyday example\nWhen using an ATM, a customer generally sees options such as:\n      Withdraw cash\n      Check balance\n      Change PIN\nThe customer does not need to understand every internal operation of the bank's\nservers to withdraw money.\nThe interface provides the relevant operations while hiding unnecessary internal\ndetails.\n5.2 Example: Student Attendance System\nSuppose we want to calculate a student's attendance percentage.\nRelevant information:\n      Classes attended\n      Total classes conducted\nFor this calculation, the student's height, home address, favourite subject, and\nblood group are not required.\nThe relevant formula is:\n\n\n\n\n                                         12\n<PARSED TEXT FOR PAGE: 13 / 32>\nThe calculation focuses only on the information needed to solve the problem.\nA complete attendance application may require additional information for other\nfeatures, such as identifying the student or generating reports.\n\n\nIn C++, abstraction is implemented mainly using classes and access specifiers,\nand also through abstract classes and pure virtual functions.\n\n\n5.3 What is relevant information?\nRelevant information directly contributes to understanding the problem, making\na decision, or producing the expected result.\nExample: Selecting a Laptop for Programming\nRelevant information may include:\n      RAM\n      Processor\n      Storage\n      Battery life\n      Price\n      Software compatibility\nLess relevant information may include the colour of the packaging, unless\nappearance is an explicit purchasing requirement.\nRelevance depends on the goal. A detail that is unnecessary for one problem may\nbe important for another.\n\n\n5.4 Abstraction versus ignoring information\nAbstraction does not mean ignoring every detail that appears unimportant at first.\nIt means identifying which details are necessary for the current purpose.\nFor example, in an attendance system:\n      Student marks may be irrelevant to attendance calculation.\n      Student identity is important when recording attendance.\n      Privacy and access control are important when designing the complete\n       system.\nGood abstraction removes unnecessary complexity without losing information\nessential to correctness, safety, or the requirements.\n\n                                         13\n<PARSED TEXT FOR PAGE: 14 / 32>\n5.5 Levels of abstraction\nA complex system can be understood at different levels.\nExample: Online Shopping System\nLevel           Focus\nHigh-level      Customer buys a product\nMiddle-level Search, cart, payment, order management\nLow-level       Database queries, validation, network requests, algorithms\n\n\nAt a high level, we focus on the overall purpose. At a lower level, we examine\nimplementation details.\n5.6 Benefits of abstraction\n      Reduces complexity\n      Improves understanding\n      Helps organize information\n      Makes design easier\n      Supports modular development\n      Helps us focus on the actual objective\n\n\n"},{"id":"representation","title":"Structured Problem Representation","text":"6. Structured Problem Representation\n6.1 What is structured problem representation?\nStructured problem representation means expressing a problem in an organized\nform so that it becomes easier to understand, analyse, communicate, and solve.\nInstead of keeping the entire problem in our mind, we represent its important\nelements using text, diagrams, tables, flowcharts, formulas, or models.\nWhy is it useful?\n      Makes the problem easier to understand.\n      Identifies inputs and outputs.\n      Shows the steps required to solve the problem.\n      Reveals relationships between components.\n      Helps identify missing requirements.\n      Makes it easier to communicate ideas to others.\n\n\n\n\n                                         14\n<PARSED TEXT FOR PAGE: 15 / 32>\n6.3 Choosing an appropriate representation\nDifferent representations serve different purposes.\nRepresentation        Best used for\nProblem statement Clearly defining the problem\nIPO model             Identifying inputs, processing, and outputs\nFlowchart             Showing the sequence of steps\nDecision tree         Showing decisions and outcomes\nTable                 Organizing or comparing information\nFormula               Expressing mathematical relationships\nBlock diagram         Showing major components and their connections\n\n\nA single problem may benefit from more than one representation.\n\n\n                                        15\n<PARSED TEXT FOR PAGE: 16 / 32>\n7. Integrated Example – College Library Book Search\nLet's apply all six Unit I topics to one real-world problem.\nProblem\nStudents spend too much time searching for books in the college library.\n1. Problem Identification and Framing\nStudents cannot easily locate the required books. The goal is to help them find\nbooks quickly.\n2. Requirements and Expected Outcomes\nStudents should search by book title or author and view availability and shelf\nlocation. The expected outcome is faster book discovery.\n3. Constraints and Assumptions\nConstraints may include budget, available computers, and development time. An\nassumption might be that the library has a digital or digitizable book catalogue.\n4. Problem Decomposition\nDivide the system into book data management, search, availability checking,\nshelf-location display, and administration.\n5. Abstraction and Relevant Information\nFocus on book title, author, availability, and shelf location. Ignore unrelated\ninformation for the basic search operation.\n6. Structured Problem Representation\nRepresent the system using an IPO model, a flowchart for searching, and tables\nfor book records and search results.\nThe example demonstrates how understanding and representing the problem\nprovides a foundation for designing a practical solution.\n\n\nA useful sequence to remember is:\nIdentify → Define → Specify → Constrain → Decompose → Abstract → Represent\n→ Solve → Verify\n\n\n\n\n                                         16\n<PARSED TEXT FOR PAGE: 17 / 32>\n   Part A: 20 questions with answers\n   Part B: 30 multiple-choice questions with answers\n   Part C: 5 small classroom tasks\n   Part D: 2 in-depth assignment tasks\n\n\n\n\n                                      17\n<PARSED TEXT FOR PAGE: 18 / 32>\n                Part A – 20 Questions and Answers\nTopic 1: Problem Identification and Framing\n1. What is a problem?\nAnswer: A problem is a situation in which we want to achieve a goal, but the\nmethod to achieve that goal is not immediately clear.\nExample: A student wants to improve examination marks but does not know how\nto plan their study.\n2. What is problem identification and problem framing?\nAnswer:\n      Problem identification means recognizing what is wrong, missing, or needs\n       improvement.\n      Problem framing means expressing the problem clearly and focusing on\n       what needs to be solved.\nExample:\nPoor framing: \"The canteen needs more counters.\"\nBetter framing: \"Students experience long waiting times in the canteen during\nlunch hours.\"\nThe better statement identifies the problem without assuming a solution.\nTopic 2: Requirements and Expected Outcomes\n3. What are requirements?\nAnswer: Requirements describe what a solution must do, provide, or satisfy.\nExample: An online library system should allow students to search for books and\ncheck their availability.\n4. Differentiate between functional and non-functional requirements.\nAnswer:\nFunctional requirements           Non-functional requirements\nDescribe what a system does       Describe how well a system works\nSearch for books                  Return results quickly\nCalculate attendance              Calculate it accurately\nGenerate examination results Protect student information\n\n\n\n\n                                         18\n<PARSED TEXT FOR PAGE: 19 / 32>\n5. What are expected outcomes and success criteria?\nAnswer:\n      Expected outcomes are the results we want to achieve.\n      Success criteria are the conditions used to verify whether those results have\n       been achieved.\nExample: The expected outcome is to reduce canteen waiting time from 12\nminutes to 6 minutes. The success criterion is that the measured average waiting\ntime reaches the target.\n6. Explain the Input–Process–Output (IPO) model with an example.\nAnswer: The IPO model represents a problem through three components:\n      Input: Data provided to the system.\n      Process: Operations performed on the data.\n      Output: Result produced by the system.\nExample: Calculate the average of three marks.\n      Input: 70, 80, 90\n      Process: Add the marks and divide by 3.\n      Output: 80\nTopic 3: Constraints and Assumptions\n7. What is a constraint? Give three examples.\nAnswer: A constraint is a limitation or restriction that a solution must satisfy.\nExamples:\n   1. Time: Complete the project in two weeks.\n   2. Budget: Develop an application within ₹10,000.\n   3. Memory: A program must operate within a specified memory limit.\n8. What is an assumption? Give an example.\nAnswer: An assumption is something accepted as true for solving a problem, even\nthough it has not been fully verified.\nExample: While calculating attendance percentage, we assume that all classes\nhave equal weight.\n\n\n\n\n                                         19\n<PARSED TEXT FOR PAGE: 20 / 32>\n9. Differentiate between constraints and assumptions.\nAnswer:\n Constraints                       Assumptions\n Limit what a solution can do      State what we temporarily accept as true\n Must be respected or addressed Should be verified when possible\n Example: Limited budget           Example: Internet access is available\n10. What is a boundary condition? Why is it important?\nAnswer: A boundary condition describes a special case at the edge of the allowed\ninput or operating range.\nIt is important because a solution that works for normal inputs may fail at the\nlimits.\nExample: If a person is eligible to vote at age 18 or above, age 18 is an important\nboundary case.\nFor an array, an empty array and a single-element array are also important\nboundary cases.\nTopic 4: Problem Decomposition\n11. What is problem decomposition?\nAnswer: Problem decomposition is the process of breaking a large or complex\nproblem into smaller, manageable subproblems.\nExample: A college management system can be divided into student registration,\nattendance, examinations, fee management, and report generation.\n12. What are the benefits of problem decomposition?\nAnswer:\n   1. Reduces complexity.\n   2. Makes planning easier.\n   3. Supports teamwork.\n   4. Makes testing easier.\n   5. Simplifies debugging.\n   6. Encourages component reuse.\n13. What is the top-down approach?\nAnswer: The top-down approach starts with the complete problem and\nprogressively divides it into smaller subproblems.\nExample:\n                                        20\n<PARSED TEXT FOR PAGE: 21 / 32>\nStudent Result System\n  |\n  |-- Read Marks\n  |-- Validate Marks\n  |-- Calculate Total\n  |-- Calculate Average\n  |-- Determine Grade\n  |-- Display Result\n14. What are dependencies between subproblems?\nAnswer: Dependencies occur when one task must be completed before another\ntask can be performed.\nExample: In a student result system, marks must be entered before validation,\nand valid marks must be available before calculating the total.\nTopic 5: Abstraction and Relevant Information\n15. What is abstraction?\nAnswer: Abstraction means focusing on the important details of a problem while\nignoring details that are not necessary for the current task.\nExample: When using an ATM, a customer can withdraw money without\nunderstanding the internal operations of the bank's servers.\n16. What is relevant information? Give an example.\nAnswer: Relevant information directly contributes to understanding a problem,\nmaking a decision, or producing the required result.\nExample: To calculate attendance percentage, the relevant information is the\nnumber of classes attended and the total number of classes conducted.\nA student's height and favourite colour are not required for this calculation.\n17. Explain the different levels of abstraction with an example.\nAnswer: A system can be understood at different levels of detail.\nExample: Online shopping system.\n      High level: A customer buys a product.\n      Middle level: Search, shopping cart, payment, and order management.\n      Low level: Database queries, validation, network requests, and algorithms.\n\n\n\n\n                                        21\n<PARSED TEXT FOR PAGE: 22 / 32>\n18. Does abstraction mean ignoring all details that appear unimportant?\nAnswer: No. Abstraction means ignoring only details that are unnecessary for the\ncurrent purpose.\nInformation that is irrelevant to one task may be essential to another.\nExample: Student marks may be irrelevant to attendance calculation, but student\nidentity is important when recording attendance.\nTopic 6: Structured Problem Representation\n19. What is structured problem representation? Name four methods.\nAnswer: Structured problem representation means expressing a problem in an\norganized form so that it becomes easier to understand, analyse, communicate,\nand solve.\nFour methods are:\n   1. Problem statement\n   2. Input–Process–Output model\n   3. Flowchart\n   4. Decision tree\nOther methods include tables, formulas, and block diagrams.\n20. Explain how to represent the problem of finding the largest of two numbers.\nAnswer:\n      Problem statement: Given two numbers, determine which is larger.\n      Input: Two numbers, A and B.\n      Process: Compare A and B.\n      Output: Display the larger number or indicate equality.\nPseudocode:\nSTART\n   Read A, B\n   IF A > B\n     PRINT \"A is larger\"\n   ELSE IF B > A\n     PRINT \"B is larger\"\n   ELSE\n     PRINT \"Both are equal\"\n   END IF\nSTOP\nThe test cases should include A greater than B, B greater than A, and A equal to\nB.\n\n\n                                        22\n<PARSED TEXT FOR PAGE: 23 / 32>\n           Part B – 30 Multiple-Choice Questions (MCQs)\nTopic 1: Problem Identification and Framing\n1. What is a problem?\nA. A situation with no goal B. A situation where the method to achieve a goal is\nnot immediately clear C. A completed task D. A programming language\nAnswer: B\n2. What should be done before selecting a solution?\nA. Start coding B. Purchase equipment C. Understand and define the problem D.\nSelect a programming language\nAnswer: C\n3. Which is the best problem statement?\nA. The canteen needs a new building. B. Students experience long waiting times\nin the canteen during lunch hours. C. Buy more computers. D. Develop an\napplication immediately.\nAnswer: B\n4. Which is an element of a good problem statement?\nA. Current situation B. Desired outcome C. Scope of the problem D. All of the\nabove\nAnswer: D\n5. What is the main purpose of problem framing?\nA. To start coding immediately B. To express the problem clearly and focus on\nwhat needs to be solved C. To ignore the causes D. To select the most expensive\nsolution\nAnswer: B\nTopic 2: Requirements and Expected Outcomes\n6. Requirements describe:\nA. Only the cost of a project B. What a solution must do, provide, or satisfy C. Only\nthe programming language D. Only the project title\nAnswer: B\n\n\n\n\n                                         23\n<PARSED TEXT FOR PAGE: 24 / 32>\n7. Which is a functional requirement?\nA. The system should be secure. B. The system should be reliable. C. Students\nshould be able to search for books. D. The system should respond quickly.\nAnswer: C\n8. Which is a non-functional requirement?\nA. Generate examination results B. Search for books C. Calculate attendance D.\nProtect user information\nAnswer: D\n9. What is an expected outcome?\nA. A limitation on the project B. A result that should be achieved C. An unverified\nassumption D. A programming error\nAnswer: B\n10. What is the purpose of success criteria?\nA. To verify whether the desired outcome has been achieved B. To increase the\nnumber of requirements C. To avoid testing D. To select a programming language\nAnswer: A\n11. In the IPO model, which component performs operations on input data?\nA. Input B. Process C. Output D. Constraint\nAnswer: B\nTopic 3: Constraints and Assumptions\n12. Which of the following is a constraint?\nA. The project must be completed within two weeks. B. The internet is available.\nC. The input data is correct. D. All users have computers.\nAnswer: A\n13. Which is an assumption?\nA. The project budget is ₹10,000. B. The project deadline is Friday. C. The internet\nconnection is available. D. Only five computers are available.\nAnswer: C\n14. What is the main difference between a constraint and an assumption?\nA. They have exactly the same meaning. B. A constraint limits a solution, while\nan assumption is accepted as true for analysis. C. An assumption always limits\nthe budget. D. A constraint is always unverified.\nAnswer: B\n\n                                         24\n<PARSED TEXT FOR PAGE: 25 / 32>\n15. If a person is eligible to vote at age 18 or above, which is an important\nboundary case?\nA. Age 10 B. Age 15 C. Age 18 D. Age 30 only\nAnswer: C\n16. Which is an important boundary case for an algorithm that finds the largest\nelement in an array?\nA. Empty array B. Single-element array C. Array with multiple elements D. All of\nthe above\nAnswer: D\nTopic 4: Problem Decomposition\n17. Problem decomposition means:\nA. Combining all tasks into one large task B. Breaking a complex problem into\nsmaller subproblems C. Ignoring difficult tasks D. Removing all requirements\nAnswer: B\n18. Which is a benefit of problem decomposition?\nA. It makes testing more difficult. B. It prevents teamwork. C. It makes complex\nproblems easier to manage. D. It eliminates the need for planning.\nAnswer: C\n19. The top-down approach starts with:\nA. The smallest task B. The complete problem C. The final output only D. A\nrandom subproblem\nAnswer: B\n20. In a student result system, which task should normally happen before\ncalculating the average?\nA. Display the final result B. Validate the marks and calculate the total C. Delete\nthe marks D. Print the grade before processing\nAnswer: B\nTopic 5: Abstraction and Relevant Information\n21. What is abstraction?\nA. Including every available detail B. Focusing on essential details and ignoring\nunnecessary ones C. Removing all information D. Making a problem more complex\nAnswer: B\n\n\n                                         25\n<PARSED TEXT FOR PAGE: 26 / 32>\n22. Which information is relevant when calculating attendance percentage?\nA. Student's favourite colour B. Student's height C. Classes attended and total\nclasses D. Student's favourite sport\nAnswer: C\n23. When using an ATM, which is an example of abstraction?\nA. The user must understand every bank server operation. B. The user can\nwithdraw cash without knowing the internal implementation. C. The user must\nwrite a program. D. The user must know the bank's database design.\nAnswer: B\n24. Which statement about abstraction is correct?\nA. All details must always be ignored. B. Only the appearance of a system matters.\nC. Relevant details depend on the purpose of the problem. D. Abstraction prevents\nproblem solving.\nAnswer: C\n25. In an online shopping system, which is a high-level view?\nA. A customer buys a product. B. A database query executes. C. A network request\nis processed. D. A variable is declared.\nAnswer: A\nTopic 6: Structured Problem Representation\n26. Which representation is best for identifying inputs, processing, and outputs?\nA. IPO model B. Decision tree only C. Paragraph of unrelated text D. Project\nbudget\nAnswer: A\n27. Which representation uses standard symbols to show the steps of an\nalgorithm?\nA. Table B. Flowchart C. Assumption D. Requirement list\nAnswer: B\n28. Which symbol is commonly used for a decision in a flowchart?\nA. Rectangle B. Oval C. Diamond D. Parallelogram\nAnswer: C\n29. Which representation is most suitable for organizing values and their\ncorresponding results?\nA. Table B. Assumption C. Problem statement only D. Constraint list\n\n                                           26\n<PARSED TEXT FOR PAGE: 27 / 32>\nAnswer: A\n30. Which sequence best represents the overall problem-solving process taught in\nUnit I?\nA. Code → Submit → Stop B. Identify → Define → Specify → Constrain →\nDecompose → Abstract → Represent → Solve → Verify C. Guess → Build → Ignore\n→ Finish D. Select → Code → Avoid testing\nAnswer: B\n\n\n\n                   Part C – 5 Small Classroom Tasks\nThese tasks are suitable for individual or pair work. Students should write their\nreasoning clearly rather than provide only the final answer.\nTask 1 – Frame the Problem\nScenario: Students complain that they cannot find the books they need in the\ncollege library.\nComplete the following:\n   1. Write the current situation.\n   2. Identify the main problem.\n   3. Identify the people affected.\n   4. State the desired outcome.\n   5. Write a clear problem statement.\n   6. Suggest      two   possible   solutions   without   assuming   that   either   is\n      automatically the best.\nTopics covered: Problem identification and framing.\nTask 2 – Identify Requirements and Constraints\nScenario: Your college wants an online assignment submission system.\nWrite:\n   1. Five functional requirements.\n   2. Three non-functional requirements.\n   3. Three constraints.\n   4. Two assumptions.\n   5. Three measurable success criteria.\nTopics covered: Requirements, expected outcomes, constraints, and assumptions.\n\n\n                                           27\n<PARSED TEXT FOR PAGE: 28 / 32>\nTask 3 – Decompose a Problem\nScenario: Develop a student result system that accepts five subject marks and\ndisplays the total, average, and grade.\nYour tasks:\n   1. Divide the problem into at least six smaller steps.\n   2. Arrange the steps in the correct order.\n   3. Identify which steps depend on earlier steps.\n   4. Identify two tasks that could be developed as separate functions.\n   5. Explain how decomposition makes testing easier.\nTopics covered: Problem decomposition and dependencies.\nTask 4 – Identify Relevant Information\nScenario: A student wants to choose a laptop for programming.\nConsider the following information:\n      RAM\n      Processor\n      Storage\n      Price\n      Battery life\n      Favourite colour\n      Packaging design\n      Software compatibility\n      Warranty\n      Brand logo size\nYour tasks:\n   1. Select the most relevant factors for the decision.\n   2. Identify the factors that are less relevant to programming requirements.\n   3. Explain why relevance depends on the goal.\n   4. Identify any important information that is missing.\nTopics covered: Abstraction and relevant information.\nTask 5 – Represent a Problem\nScenario: Given two numbers, determine the larger number or report that both\nare equal.\nYour tasks:\n\n                                          28\n<PARSED TEXT FOR PAGE: 29 / 32>\n   1. Write a problem statement.\n   2. Identify the inputs, processing, and outputs.\n   3. Create a comparison table containing at least four test cases.\n   4. Draw a flowchart.\n   5. Write pseudocode.\n   6. Include a test case where both numbers are equal.\nTopics covered: Structured problem representation and boundary cases.\n\n\n\n              Part D – 2 In-Depth Assignment Tasks\nAssignment 1 – Smart College Library Search System\nTopics: All six Unit I topics\nProblem statement\nStudents spend too much time finding books in the college library. The college\nwants to develop a system that allows students to search for books, check\navailability, and identify shelf locations.\nAssignment requirements\nTask 1: Problem identification and framing\n      Describe the current situation.\n      Identify the problem and affected stakeholders.\n      Write a clear problem statement.\n      Define the scope of the proposed solution.\nTask 2: Requirements and expected outcomes\n      Identify at least six functional requirements.\n      Identify at least four non-functional requirements.\n      Define five expected outcomes or success criteria.\nTask 3: Constraints and assumptions\n      Identify at least four constraints.\n      Identify at least three assumptions.\n      Identify at least three boundary or exceptional cases.\nTask 4: Problem decomposition\n      Divide the system into at least six modules.\n      Explain the purpose of each module.\n\n\n                                             29\n<PARSED TEXT FOR PAGE: 30 / 32>\n      Identify dependencies between modules.\n      Draw a top-down decomposition diagram.\nTask 5: Abstraction and relevant information\n      Identify the information required for searching books.\n      Identify information that is unnecessary for the basic search operation.\n      Explain which information may become relevant to other library functions.\nTask 6: Structured problem representation Prepare the following:\n   1. Problem statement\n   2. Input–Process–Output model\n   3. Flowchart for searching a book\n   4. Table containing sample book records\n   5. Decision tree for checking book availability\n   6. A simple representation of the overall system\nSubmission format\n      Written report: 5–8 pages\n      Diagrams: At least three\n      Presentation: 5 minutes\n      Include a conclusion explaining how clear problem understanding can\n       prevent design mistakes.\nAssignment 2 – Student Result Analysis System\nTopics:    Requirements,     constraints,      decomposition,   abstraction,      and\nrepresentation\nProblem statement\nA college currently calculates student results manually. The process takes time\nand may produce calculation errors. Design a structured solution that accepts\nsubject marks, validates them, calculates totals and averages, determines grades\naccording to supplied rules, and displays results.\nAssignment requirements\nTask 1: Define the problem\n      Write a clear problem statement.\n      Identify inputs, outputs, and expected outcomes.\n      Specify the scope of the system.\n\n\n                                          30\n<PARSED TEXT FOR PAGE: 31 / 32>\nTask 2: Define the rules\nUse the following illustrative rules:\n      The student has five subjects.\n      Each subject is marked out of 100.\n      Valid marks range from 0 to 100, inclusive.\n      Total marks are the sum of the five subject marks.\n      Average marks equal total marks divided by five.\nFor the assignment, define a simple grading scheme and state it explicitly. Do not\nassume it is an official college grading policy.\nTask 3: Identify constraints and assumptions\n      List at least four constraints.\n      List at least three assumptions.\n      Explain how invalid marks should be handled.\n      Consider missing marks and the minimum and maximum valid marks.\nTask 4: Decompose the problem Divide the system into smaller tasks, such as:\n      Read student details.\n      Read subject marks.\n      Validate marks.\n      Calculate total.\n      Calculate average.\n      Determine grade.\n      Display results.\nExplain the dependencies between these tasks.\nTask 5: Apply abstraction\n      Identify information essential for calculating the result.\n      Identify information needed only for student identification or reporting.\n      Identify information unnecessary for the calculation itself.\nTask 6: Structured representation Prepare:\n   1. IPO model\n   2. Flowchart\n   3. Pseudocode\n   4. Sample result table\n   5. Test cases\n\n                                          31\n<PARSED TEXT FOR PAGE: 32 / 32>\n   6. A decomposition diagram\nRequired test cases\nInclude at least these situations:\n      All subjects have high marks.\n      All subjects have zero marks.\n      One subject has the maximum mark of 100.\n      One subject has an invalid mark, such as 105.\n      One subject has a negative mark.\n      All five marks are valid but different.\n\n\n\n\n                                          32\n"}]},{"subjectId":"pea103","title":"The Complete AI Ecosystem","sourceFormat":"pdf","sourceUrl":"resources/PEA103-AI-Interaction-Design.pdf","topics":[{"id":"ai-foundations","title":"Foundations","pages":[{"number":1,"text":"A visual reference guide · 2026\r\n\r\nThe Complete\r\nAI Ecosystem\r\n\r\nData, Models, Local AI, Infrastructure, Applications, and Responsible AI\r\n\r\n01  02  03  04  05  06  07  08  09                                        10  11  12  13  14  15\r\n\r\n15 connected layers · from raw data to production, governed end to end\r\n\f"},{"number":2,"text":"Master ecosystem map · 15 layers\r\n\r\nHow the 15 layers connect\r\n\r\n01                       02           03                         04                    05                                     06\r\nData sources                          Storage & retrieval\r\n                         Ingestion &                             Quality, prep & data  AI & model                             Model families &\r\n                         integration                             governance            development                            capabilities\r\n\r\n12                       08                      07              13                                                           14\r\n                         Deployment & inference                  Connected systems\r\nFoundation, open-weight                          AI application                                                               People, users &\r\n& local models                                   architecture                                                                 industries\r\n\r\n09                                    10                                               11\r\n                                      Compute, cloud, hardware & infrastructure        AI tools & developer ecosystem\r\nMLOps, LLMOps & observability — the\r\nfeedback loop back to 01\r\n\r\n15 · Governance, safety, security & responsible AI — a continuous control layer across every layer above, never a final step\r\n\r\nThe Complete AI Ecosystem · 02\r\n\f"},{"number":3,"text":"The Complete AI Ecosystem\r\n\r\nSECTION 01\r\n\r\nFoundations\r\n\r\nThe vocabulary of AI, and the parts every AI system is built from.\r\n\r\nThe AI landscape · AI system architecture\r\n\f"},{"number":4,"text":"§1 Foundations                                                      Foundation models\r\n\r\nUnderstanding the AI landscape                                      Large models pre-trained on broad data, then adapted to many tasks. Most\r\n                                                                    generative AI is built on them.\r\n   Artificial intelligence — systems that perceive, reason, decide\r\n   or act                                                           LLMs and SLMs\r\n\r\n      Machine learning — learns patterns from data instead of       Large language models maximize capability. Small language models, typically a\r\n      hand-written rules                                            few billion parameters, trade breadth for speed, cost and on-device use.\r\n\r\n         Deep learning — many-layered neural networks               Traditional vs generative\r\n\r\n            Generative AI — creates new text, images, audio,        Traditional ML predicts or classifies — a fraud score, a demand forecast.\r\n            video and code                                          Generative AI produces new content. Real systems often combine both.\r\n\r\n   Takeaway — the terms nest; they are not synonyms. All generative AI is deep learning, but much production AI is still classical machine learning.\r\nThe Complete AI Ecosystem · 04\r\n\f"},{"number":5,"text":"§1 Foundations\r\n\r\nAI system architecture\r\n\r\nSeven parts: data · models · orchestration · inference · application · users · governance\r\n\r\nConcept         What it is                                                              Example\r\nModel           Trained weights that turn inputs into outputs                           An open-weight LLM file; a fraud classifier\r\nModel API       A hosted endpoint that runs a model for you, billed per use             A provider’s chat or messages endpoint\r\nAI application  Software wrapping a model with prompts, data, interface and business    A support chatbot; a meeting summarizer\r\n                rules\r\nAI agent        An application where the model plans and acts through tools, in a loop  Triages tickets and updates the CRM\r\nAI platform     Shared infrastructure to build, serve, evaluate and govern many models  Managed hosting with evals, monitoring and\r\n                and apps                                                                access control\r\n\r\n   Takeaway — a model is a component, not a product. The application around it decides most of the quality, cost and risk.\r\nThe Complete AI Ecosystem · 05\r\n\f"}]},{"id":"ai-data","title":"Data & Knowledge","pages":[{"number":6,"text":"The Complete AI Ecosystem\r\n\r\nSECTION 02 · LAYERS 01–04\r\n\r\nData & Knowledge\r\n\r\nWhere AI gets its raw material, and how that material becomes usable.\r\n\r\nData sources · Ingestion · Storage · Retrieval stores · Preparation\r\n\f"},{"number":7,"text":"§2 Data & Knowledge · Layer 01\r\n\r\nData sources and collection\r\n\r\nAI consumes five shapes of data: structured, semi-structured, unstructured, streaming and multimodal.\r\n\r\nStructured                              Semi-structured                         Unstructured\r\n\r\nMySQL · PostgreSQL · SQL Server · data  MongoDB · Cassandra · DynamoDB · JSON   Websites · PDFs · books · papers · code &\r\nwarehouses · enterprise records         documents · application logs            docs · knowledge bases\r\n\r\nMultimodal                              Streaming                               Human & synthetic\r\n\r\nImages · video · audio · speech ·       APIs · telemetry · event streams · IoT  User content & feedback · open datasets ·\r\nscanned documents                       sensors · edge systems                  synthetic data · simulations\r\n\r\n   Takeaway — know each source’s owner, license and refresh rate before it feeds a model; those three decide what you may build on it.\r\nThe Complete AI Ecosystem · 07\r\n\f"},{"number":8,"text":"§2 Data & Knowledge · Layer 02\r\n\r\nData ingestion, ETL/ELT and streaming\r\n\r\nIngestion moves data from sources into storage, checking it on the way in.\r\n\r\nSource  →  Extract                 →  Validate                       →  Transform  →                     Load              →  Catalog\r\n\r\nBatch                              Real-time                            Connectors                                         Controls\r\n\r\nScheduled bulk loads. ETL          Streaming, Change Data               API integrations, web crawling,                    Schema enforcement,\r\ntransforms before loading; ELT     Capture from databases, event-       document ingestion. AWS Glue ·                     validation, deduplication, data\r\nloads first and transforms in the  driven pipelines. Apache Kafka ·     Azure Data Factory · Google                        contracts, lineage and\r\nwarehouse. Spark · Airflow · dbt   Apache Flink                         Cloud Dataflow                                     provenance\r\n\r\n   Takeaway — reject bad data at the door. Every record that slips past validation can resurface later as a wrong answer.\r\nThe Complete AI Ecosystem · 08\r\n\f"},{"number":9,"text":"§2 Data & Knowledge · Layer 03\r\n\r\nData lakes, lakehouses, warehouses and databases\r\n\r\nStore               Best for                                               Examples\r\nObject storage      Raw files, model artifacts, backups at any scale       Amazon S3 · Azure Blob · Google Cloud Storage\r\nLake / lakehouse    Open-format files with table features and big compute  Databricks · Apache Iceberg tables\r\nData warehouse      Governed analytical SQL and BI                         Snowflake · BigQuery · ClickHouse\r\nRelational / NoSQL  Exact, transactional application records               PostgreSQL · MySQL · MongoDB · Redis\r\nTime-series         Metrics and sensor readings over time                  TimescaleDB · InfluxDB\r\nFeature store       The same ML features for training and serving          Feast\r\nMetadata catalog    Discovery, ownership, lineage, access policy           Unity Catalog · DataHub\r\n\r\n   Takeaway — pick storage by access pattern: files, analytics or transactions. Retention rules and access control apply to all of them.\r\nThe Complete AI Ecosystem · 09\r\n\f"},{"number":10,"text":"§2 Data & Knowledge · Layer 03\r\n\r\nVector, search, graph and SQL stores are not interchangeable\r\n\r\nEach answers a different kind of question. Most RAG systems combine two or more.\r\n\r\nVector database             Full-text search            Graph database                   Relational / SQL\r\n\r\nFinds similar meaning       Finds exact words, ranked   Finds relationships across hops  Finds exact records and totals\r\n                            “error code E-4031”                                          “refunds in March by region”\r\n“passages about             Elasticsearch · OpenSearch  “which suppliers feed recalled   PostgreSQL · MySQL\r\nrefund disputes”                                        products?”\r\n\r\nQdrant · Milvus · Weaviate                              Neo4j · Amazon Neptune ·\r\n· pgvector                                              knowledge graphs\r\n\r\n   Takeaway — hybrid search (vector + keyword) is the common default; object storage keeps the original files these indexes point to.\r\nThe Complete AI Ecosystem · 10\r\n\f"},{"number":11,"text":"§2 Data & Knowledge · Layer 04\r\n\r\nData quality, preparation and governance\r\n\r\nClean                    Shape              Index          Protect              Trust                                         Split\r\n\r\nNormalize,               Tokenize, label,   Parse, chunk,  Redact PII, control  Check bias, license,                          Train, validation,\r\ndeduplicate,             engineer features  generate       access, retain       provenance                                    test; version it\r\ntest quality                                embeddings\r\n\r\nPurpose                  Data needed                       Key preparation\r\nClassical ML             Labeled tables                    Features, labels, leak-free splits\r\nFoundation pre-training  Trillions of tokens               Filtering, deduplication, contamination checks, licensing\r\nFine-tuning              Curated examples                  Instruction and preference pairs, PII removal, versioning\r\nRAG                      Your documents                    Parsing, chunking, embeddings, metadata and access tags\r\n\r\n   Takeaway — preparation depends on the goal: training needs scale and cleanliness; RAG needs good parsing and permissions.\r\nThe Complete AI Ecosystem · 11\r\n\f"}]},{"id":"ai-models","title":"Models & Training","pages":[{"number":12,"text":"The Complete AI Ecosystem\r\n\r\nSECTION 03 · LAYERS 05, 06, 12\r\n\r\nModels & Training\r\n\r\nHow models are built, adapted, chosen and accessed.\r\n\r\nDevelopment · Training pathways · RAG vs fine-tuning · Model families · Multimodal · Model access\r\n\f"},{"number":13,"text":"§3 Models & Training · Layer 05\r\n\r\nThe model development lifecycle\r\n\r\n01                             02                           03                               04\r\n\r\nDefine                         Explore                      Engineer                         Train or adapt\r\n\r\nProblem, success metric and a  Statistical analysis, EDA,   Features and labels, or prompts  Supervised, unsupervised, self-\r\nsimple baseline                data checks                  and context                      supervised or RL\r\n\r\n05                             06                           07                               08\r\n\r\nTune                           Evaluate                     Register                         Optimize\r\n\r\nHyperparameter search,         Held-out tests, task evals,  Approval, model registry,        Distill or quantize, then hand off\r\ntracked experiments            safety and bias checks       versioned artifacts              to serving\r\n\r\n   Takeaway — start from a baseline and a metric. Without them you cannot tell whether a bigger model or more training actually helped.\r\nThe Complete AI Ecosystem · 13\r\n\f"},{"number":14,"text":"§3 Models & Training · Deep dive 4.4\r\n\r\nSix ways to get a working model\r\n\r\nWorkflow            Compute                              Data                             Watch for\r\nTrain from scratch  Large GPU or TPU clusters, weeks to  Billions to trillions of tokens  Checkpointing, evals at scale, data licensing\r\n                    months\r\nFull fine-tune      Several GPUs, hours to days          Thousands or more examples       Base-model license, forgetting old skills\r\nPEFT: LoRA, QLoRA   One or a few GPUs                    Hundreds to thousands of         Small adapters; QLoRA trains on a 4-bit base\r\n                                                         examples\r\nRAG                 Embedding and inference only         Your documents                   Retrieval quality, freshness, permissions\r\nPrompt-only         Inference only                       A few examples                   Fastest start; capped by the base model\r\nDistill, quantize   Teacher inference plus student       Teacher outputs                  Quality loss; terms on using teacher outputs\r\n                    training\r\n\r\nThe Complete AI Ecosystem · 14\r\n\f"},{"number":15,"text":"§3 Models & Training · Deep dive 4.1\r\n\r\nPrompting, RAG, fine-tuning or long context?\r\n\r\nChanges    Prompting             RAG                           Fine-tuning                                      Long context\r\nBest for   Instructions only     Knowledge at query time       Behavior, format, style                          How much fits in one request\r\n           Quick, general tasks  Private, changing facts with  Consistent output and                            A few large documents\r\nFreshness                        citations                     domain skills\r\nCost       Instant               As fresh as the index         Frozen at training time                          Per request\r\nMain risk  Lowest                Indexing plus retrieval       Training run plus hosting                        Many tokens per call\r\n           Brittle prompts       Poor retrieval, wrong         Stale facts, lost skills                         Cost; details lost mid-context\r\n                                 answers\r\n\r\n   Takeaway — use RAG for what the model should know, fine-tuning for how it should behave. They combine well.\r\nThe Complete AI Ecosystem · 15\r\n\f"},{"number":16,"text":"§3 Models & Training · Layer 06\r\n\r\nModel families: general-purpose and specialized\r\n\r\nGeneral-purpose                 Perception                  Generation                  Retrieval\r\n\r\nLarge language models           Vision · detection &        Text-to-speech · image ·    Text and image embeddings\r\nSmall language models           segmentation · speech       video · diffusion models    · rerankers\r\nMultimodal models               recognition\r\nCode-generation models                                      Ranking & graph             Physical\r\n                                Predictive\r\nOne model, many tasks, steered                              Recommendation & ranking ·  Robotics & action ·\r\nby prompts.                     Classification ·            graph ML                    geospatial · scientific\r\n                                regression · forecasting ·\r\n                                anomaly detection\r\n\r\n   Takeaway — specialized models are usually smaller, cheaper and more accurate on their one task; general models trade that for breadth.\r\nThe Complete AI Ecosystem · 16\r\n\f"},{"number":17,"text":"§3 Models & Training · Deep dive 4.5\r\n\r\nMultimodal AI\r\n\r\nEncoders turn each input into tokens or embeddings that one model can reason over.\r\n\r\nText                     Encoders                  Embeddings            Fusion                Decoders\r\nImages            → One per modality,       → A shared             → Attention across   → Text, speech, image\r\nAudio & speech\r\nVideo                      plus tokenizers           vector space           modalities           or video out\r\nDocuments\r\nSensors & motion\r\n\r\n   Takeaway — support varies by model: many read images but cannot produce them. Check each model’s input and output modalities.\r\nThe Complete AI Ecosystem · 17\r\n\f"},{"number":18,"text":"§3 Models & Training · Layer 12\r\n\r\nFour ways to access a foundation model\r\n\r\nExamples   A · Proprietary API     B · Open-weight           C · Local                                                      D · Cloud-hosted open\r\nRuns on                                                      Ollama · LM Studio ·                                           Bedrock · Microsoft Foundry ·\r\n           OpenAI GPT · Anthropic  Llama · Qwen · Mistral ·  llama.cpp · MLX                                                Vertex AI · Together\r\n           Claude · Google Gemini  DeepSeek · Gemma · Phi    Your own machine                                               A managed host or your\r\n                                                                                                                            cloud GPUs\r\n           The provider’s cloud    Wherever you choose       Hardware you own                                               Per token or GPU-hour\r\n\r\nPricing    Per token               Free weights; you pay     Data stays on device                                           Depends on the host\r\n                                   compute                   RAM and VRAM limits                                            Region, SLAs, egress\r\nPrivacy    Provider terms apply\r\nWatch for  Lock-in, deprecations   Full control of weights\r\n\r\n                                   License terms vary\r\n\r\n   Takeaway — open-weight describes the license, not the location. The same model can run on a laptop or in a data center.\r\nThe Complete AI Ecosystem · 18\r\n\f"},{"number":19,"text":"§3 Models & Training · Layer 12B\r\n\r\nOpen-weight model families and their licenses\r\n\r\nFamily    Developer   Typical license                     Notes\r\nLlama     Meta        Llama community license (custom)    Use policy; extra terms for huge services\r\nQwen      Alibaba     Mostly Apache 2.0                   Wide size range; multilingual, coding\r\nMistral   Mistral AI  Apache 2.0 for many; others custom  Check each model’s terms\r\nDeepSeek  DeepSeek    MIT for recent releases             Reasoning; mixture-of-experts\r\nGemma     Google      Gemma terms of use (custom)         Small to mid sizes; on-device\r\nPhi       Microsoft   MIT                                 Small language models\r\ngpt-oss   OpenAI      Apache 2.0                          Open-weight reasoning models\r\nOLMo      Ai2         Apache 2.0                          Fully open: weights, data, code\r\n\r\nThe Complete AI Ecosystem · 19 · Licenses change: verify each model and version\r\n\f"}]},{"id":"ai-local","title":"Local AI","pages":[{"number":20,"text":"The Complete AI Ecosystem\r\n\r\nSECTION 04 · LAYER 12C\r\n\r\nLocal AI\r\n\r\nRunning models on your own laptops, workstations and servers.\r\n\r\nTools and runtimes · Local architecture · Hardware and memory\r\n\f"},{"number":21,"text":"§4 Local AI · Layer 12C\r\n\r\nLocal AI tools play different roles\r\n\r\nTool           Role                      Best for\r\nLM Studio      Desktop app               Browse, download and chat with models\r\nJan · GPT4All  Desktop apps              Private offline chat, local documents\r\nOllama         Model manager, local API  One-command models with a localhost API\r\nLocalAI        Local API server          OpenAI-compatible API over many backends\r\nllama.cpp      Inference runtime         GGUF models on CPU, Metal, CUDA, ROCm\r\nMLX            Apple Silicon runtime     Fast inference and fine-tuning on Macs\r\nTransformers   Python library            Research, custom pipelines, fine-tuning\r\nvLLM · SGLang  Production serving        High-throughput, multi-user GPU serving\r\n\r\n   Takeaway — apps sit on top of runtimes: LM Studio and Ollama both build on llama.cpp-style engines.\r\nThe Complete AI Ecosystem · 21\r\n\f"},{"number":22,"text":"§4 Local AI · Deep dive 4.3\r\n\r\nLocal AI architecture\r\n\r\nInterface → Local app →                         Local API   Model load                      Hardware                                   Output\r\n                                                runtime on\r\nchat, IDE, app  LM Studio, Jan                   localhost  → GGUF or MLX → CPU, GPU, Apple →                                          on your device\r\n\r\n                                                            weights                         Silicon\r\n\r\nOptional add-ons                                Fully offline                               May go online\r\n\r\nLocal embeddings · local vector database ·      Inference · embeddings · vector search and  Model downloads and updates · telemetry ·\r\ndocument ingestion · local RAG · local tools ·  RAG over local files                        web tools · remote API fallback\r\napp integrations\r\n\r\n   Takeaway — “local” is a privacy boundary. Check each app’s telemetry and fallback settings before trusting it with sensitive data.\r\nThe Complete AI Ecosystem · 22\r\n\f"},{"number":23,"text":"§4 Local AI · Hardware\r\n\r\nHow much memory a local model needs\r\n\r\nMemory ≈ parameters × bytes per weight, plus room for context (KV cache).\r\n\r\nModel size     16-bit     8-bit  4-bit (GGUF)    Typically fits on\r\n3–4B           ~8 GB    ~4 GB         ~2–3 GB    Most laptops, CPU-only machines, some phones\r\n7–8B         ~16 GB     ~8 GB             ~5 GB  16 GB laptops, entry-level GPUs\r\n13–14B       ~28 GB    ~14 GB         ~8–9 GB    16 GB+ Macs, 12–16 GB GPUs\r\n30–32B       ~64 GB    ~32 GB                    24 GB GPUs, 32 GB+ Macs\r\n70B         ~140 GB    ~70 GB      ~18–20 GB     48 GB+ GPUs, 64 GB+ Macs, workstations\r\n                                        ~40 GB\r\n\r\n   Takeaway — 4-bit quantization is the usual sweet spot. Weights-only figures: leave headroom for long context.\r\nThe Complete AI Ecosystem · 23\r\n\f"}]},{"id":"ai-applications","title":"Applications, RAG & Agents","pages":[{"number":24,"text":"The Complete AI Ecosystem\r\n\r\nSECTION 05 · LAYERS 07, 13, 14\r\n\r\nApplications, RAG & Agents\r\n\r\nHow models become products that people and systems actually use.\r\n\r\nApp architecture · App types · RAG · Agents · Connected systems · People\r\n\f"},{"number":25,"text":"§5 Applications · Layer 07\r\n\r\nInside an AI application\r\n\r\n User interface            Guardrails           Orchestrator               Memory          The orchestrator\r\nchat · voice · app  input & output checks                           short- & long-term     assembles context, calls\r\n                                              system prompt                                the model and runs\r\n                                           conversation context           Retrieval        permitted tools.\r\n                                                                 vector · keyword · graph\r\n                                              state & policies                                  External systems\r\n                                                 tool routing                Model              APIs · permissions\r\n                                                                 LLM · SLM · multimodal\r\n\r\n                                                                             Tools\r\n                                                                     registry · sandbox\r\n\r\n   Takeaway — the model is one box of many. Quality and safety come from how the orchestrator, retrieval and guardrails are built.\r\nThe Complete AI Ecosystem · 25\r\n\f"},{"number":26,"text":"§5 Applications · Layer 07\r\n\r\nNot every chatbot is an agent; not every app is RAG\r\n\r\nEach step adds one capability, and with it more power and more risk.\r\n\r\nType         Adds                                Example                               Takes actions?\r\nChatbot      Model, system prompt, conversation  FAQ assistant                         No\r\nRAG app      Retrieval and citations             Policy Q&A over company docs          No\r\nCopilot      In-product context and suggestions  Code completion in an IDE             No — the person acts\r\nAgent        Tools, planning, an action loop     Resolves a support ticket end to end  Yes, within permissions\r\nMulti-agent  Several agents and a coordinator    Research, draft, review pipeline      Yes, coordinated\r\n\r\n   Takeaway — choose the simplest type that solves the problem. Autonomy should be earned through evaluation, not assumed.\r\nThe Complete AI Ecosystem · 26\r\n\f"},{"number":27,"text":"§5 Applications · Deep dive 4.1\r\n\r\nThe Retrieval-Augmented Generation pipeline\r\n\r\nINDEXING · AHEAD OF TIME\r\n\r\nSources  →  Ingest          →       Parse  →  Chunk  →  Embed                                                                    →  Index\r\n\r\nQUERY TIME · EVERY REQUEST\r\n\r\nQuery    → Retrieve →       Rerank  → Build context → Generate → Check citations →                                                  Answer\r\n\r\n   Takeaway — most RAG failures are retrieval failures. If the right passage never reaches the model, no model can answer well.\r\nThe Complete AI Ecosystem · 27\r\n\f"},{"number":28,"text":"§5 Applications · Deep dive 4.1\r\n\r\nTechniques that make retrieval work\r\n\r\nDense retrieval          Sparse retrieval      Hybrid search             Metadata filters          Query rewriting\r\n                                                                                                   Clarify the question;\r\nEmbeddings match         Keyword scoring for   Dense and sparse results  Limit by date, source or  run variants\r\nmeaning, not words       exact terms           combined                  permission\r\n                                                                                                   Retrieval evals\r\nReranking                Graph retrieval       Compression               Citation checks           Recall, precision,\r\n                                                                                                   faithfulness\r\nA second model reorders  Follows entity links  Trims context to          Each claim backed by\r\ntop hits                 across hops           what matters              a source\r\n\r\n   Takeaway — measure retrieval separately from generation. A labeled set of questions and correct sources is the most valuable RAG asset.\r\nThe Complete AI Ecosystem · 28\r\n\f"},{"number":29,"text":"§5 Applications · Deep dive 4.2\r\n\r\nThe agent loop\r\n\r\n01                  → 02                  → 03                       → 04                                         → 05\r\nUser request\r\n                           Plan the task         Select a tool              Check permission                             Execute\r\n\r\n06                  → 07                  → 08                       → 09                                         → 10\r\nObserve the result\r\n                           Update state          Choose next action         Verify the outcome                           Done, or ask a human\r\n\r\n↺ Steps 02–08 repeat until the goal is verified or a budget, time or iteration limit is reached.\r\n\r\n   Takeaway — the permission check (04) and verification (09) are what separate a useful agent from a risky one.\r\nThe Complete AI Ecosystem · 29\r\n\f"},{"number":30,"text":"§5 Applications · Deep dive 4.2\r\n\r\nWhat a production agent needs\r\n\r\nComponent                        Why it matters\r\nFunction calling, tool registry  The model chooses only from declared, typed tools\r\nScoped permissions               Least privilege per tool and per user\r\nSandboxed execution              Generated code runs isolated from real systems\r\nHuman approval                   Required for irreversible or high-impact actions\r\nMemory and state                 Resume work and carry context across steps\r\nWorkflow graphs                  Fixed steps where reliability matters more than freedom\r\nRetries, timeouts, budgets       Caps on iterations, spend and time\r\nAudit logging                    Every plan, tool call and result recorded\r\nEvaluation and red teaming       Test task success and misuse before release\r\n\r\n   Takeaway — give agents the narrowest permissions that still get the job done, and log everything they do.\r\nThe Complete AI Ecosystem · 30\r\n\f"},{"number":31,"text":"§5 Applications · Layer 13\r\n\r\nConnected systems and enterprise applications\r\n\r\nAI application         Integration layer                         CRM        ERP        Payments\r\n    or agent                                                                Calendar   Support\r\n                         APIs and connectors (e.g. MCP)          Email      Wikis      Identity\r\n                                                                            Dev tools  SecOps\r\n                ↔ Scoped permissions                     ↔\r\n\r\n                         Business rules                          Documents\r\n                         Audit trail\r\n                                                                 BI\r\n\r\nAcross industries — software engineering · education · healthcare · finance · manufacturing · retail & e-commerce · government & public services\r\n\r\n   Takeaway — the AI should act with the user’s permissions, never more, and every write to a business system should leave an audit trail.\r\nThe Complete AI Ecosystem · 31\r\n\f"},{"number":32,"text":"§5 Applications · Layer 14\r\n\r\nPeople, users and industries\r\n\r\nBuilders                         Business                      Domain teams                  Everyone\r\n\r\nSoftware engineers · AI/ML       Business analysts ·           Healthcare · finance ·        Students and educators ·\r\nengineers · data scientists ·    product managers · owners     manufacturing · security      everyday users\r\ndata engineers · researchers     and executives                · customer support ·\r\n                                                               public sector                 Oversight\r\nHOW PEOPLE SHAPE AI              Feedback\r\n                                                               Expertise                     Approvals and accountability\r\n  Requirements                   Ratings and corrections feed\r\n                                 Layer 09                      Labels, evals and edge cases\r\n   Define what a good result is\r\n\r\nThe Complete AI Ecosystem · 32\r\n\f"}]},{"id":"ai-production","title":"Production & Infrastructure","pages":[{"number":33,"text":"The Complete AI Ecosystem\r\n\r\nSECTION 06 · LAYERS 08–11\r\n\r\nProduction & Infrastructure\r\n\r\nServing models reliably, watching them, and the compute underneath.\r\n\r\nDeployment · Serving · MLOps · Observability · Compute · Compute stack · Developer tools\r\n\f"},{"number":34,"text":"§6 Production · Layer 08\r\n\r\nDeployment and inference: where models run\r\n\r\nExposed through REST or GraphQL APIs, streaming or batch, behind authentication, rate limits and quotas.\r\n\r\nOption                  Runs in                    Choose when\r\nCloud inference API     Provider data centers      Fastest start; frontier models\r\nSelf-hosted in cloud    Your cloud GPUs            Open models, control, steady high volume\r\nOn-prem, private cloud  Your data center           Data residency or strict regulation\r\nLocal PC, workstation   The user’s machine         Privacy, offline use, prototyping\r\nEdge and mobile         Phones, cameras, vehicles  Low latency or no connectivity\r\n\r\n   Takeaway — many teams mix options: a cloud API for hard tasks, a small self-hosted or local model for cheap, private or offline ones.\r\nThe Complete AI Ecosystem · 34\r\n\f"},{"number":35,"text":"§6 Production · Layer 08\r\n\r\nEfficient serving, and why training is different\r\n\r\nBatching          Caching         Quantization                Routing            Autoscaling        Fallbacks\r\nShare GPU passes  Reuse prompts,  Fewer bits                  Small model first  Load-balanced      Backup model\r\n                  results         per weight                                     replicas           on failure\r\n\r\nGoal              Training infrastructure                     Inference infrastructure\r\nHardware          Throughput over long jobs                   Low latency per request\r\nLoad              Multi-node GPU clusters, fast interconnect  Right-sized GPUs, CPUs or edge chips\r\nBottleneck        Scheduled runs lasting days or weeks        Always on, follows user traffic\r\nCost lever        Compute and interconnect                    Memory and the KV cache\r\n                  Utilization, checkpointing                  Batching, caching, quantization\r\n\r\nThe Complete AI Ecosystem · 35\r\n\f"},{"number":36,"text":"§6 Production · Layer 09\r\n\r\nMLOps and LLMOps: the continuous improvement loop\r\n\r\nData  →                           Develop  →  Evaluate               →  Deploy  →                       Monitor          →  Feedback\r\n\r\n↺ Feedback returns to Data — the loop never ends while the system is live.\r\n\r\nBuild                                      Evaluate                     Release                         Respond\r\n\r\nCI/CD · infrastructure as code ·           Offline and online evals     Canary deployments · A/B tests  Incident response · model\r\nversioned models, datasets,                · regression tests ·         · fast rollbacks                retirement and replacement\r\nprompts, configs                           factuality checks\r\n\r\n   Takeaway — version prompts like code. In LLM apps a one-line prompt change can shift quality as much as a new model.\r\nThe Complete AI Ecosystem · 36\r\n\f"},{"number":37,"text":"§6 Production · Layer 09\r\n\r\nWhat to observe in production\r\n\r\nSignal      What to track                             Why\r\nLatency     Time to first token, total response time  User experience\r\nThroughput  Requests and tokens per second            Capacity planning\r\nCost        Tokens and GPU-hours per task             Unit economics\r\nGPU use     Memory and compute utilization            Right-sizing\r\nQuality     Eval scores, hallucination checks         Catch regressions\r\nRetrieval   Hit rate, source relevance                RAG health\r\nDrift       Shifts in inputs and outputs              Retraining triggers\r\nFeedback    Ratings, corrections, escalations         Ground truth\r\nTraces      Prompts, tool calls, responses            Debugging, audits\r\n\r\n   Takeaway — track cost per successful task, not just cost per token; a cheap model that fails often is expensive.\r\nThe Complete AI Ecosystem · 37\r\n\f"},{"number":38,"text":"§6 Production · Layer 10\r\n\r\nCompute, cloud, hardware and infrastructure\r\n\r\nCloud providers                        Compute                                Vendors\r\n\r\nAWS · Microsoft Azure · Google Cloud   CPUs · GPUs · TPUs · NPUs · other      NVIDIA · AMD · Intel · Google TPU · Apple\r\n· Oracle Cloud · GPU clouds such       accelerators · multi-GPU, multi-       Silicon · AWS Trainium · Cerebras\r\nas CoreWeave                           node clusters\r\n                                                                              Operations\r\nPlatform                               Security\r\n                                                                              Autoscaling · monitoring · cost management\r\nVMs · Docker containers · Kubernetes   IAM · secrets management · backup and  and FinOps\r\n· serverless · storage · networking ·  disaster recovery\r\nload balancers\r\n\r\n   Takeaway — GPU capacity is often the scarcest resource. Plan availability, region and cost before choosing a model size.\r\nThe Complete AI Ecosystem · 38\r\n\f"},{"number":39,"text":"§6 Production · Layer 10\r\n\r\nThe AI compute stack, from silicon to application\r\n\r\nApplications          APIs · apps · agents\r\n\r\nModel serving         vLLM · SGLang · NVIDIA Triton · Ollama\r\n\r\nInference runtimes    TensorRT · ONNX Runtime · OpenVINO · MLX · llama.cpp\r\n\r\nML frameworks         PyTorch · JAX · TensorFlow\r\n\r\nCompute platforms     CUDA (NVIDIA) · ROCm (AMD) · Metal (Apple) · oneAPI (Intel)\r\n\r\nDrivers and firmware  Vendor GPU and NPU drivers\r\n\r\nHardware              CPU · GPU · TPU · NPU\r\n\r\n   Takeaway — each layer depends on the one below. A runtime that lacks support for your hardware rules out that hardware.\r\nThe Complete AI Ecosystem · 39\r\n\f"},{"number":40,"text":"§6 Production · Layer 11\r\n\r\nAI tools and the developer ecosystem\r\n\r\nCategory        Examples                                  Category          Examples\r\nEnvironments    Python · Jupyter · VS Code · Git          Prompts           MLflow · Langfuse prompt registries\r\nHugging Face    Hub · Transformers · Datasets · PEFT ·    Vector search     Qdrant · Milvus · Weaviate · pgvector ·\r\n                Accelerate                                                  FAISS\r\nFrameworks      PyTorch · TensorFlow · JAX                Annotation        Label Studio · Argilla\r\nExperiments     MLflow · Weights & Biases                 Synthetic data    Distilabel\r\nOrchestration   Airflow · Kubeflow · Prefect · Dagster    Serving           vLLM · SGLang · Triton · Ray Serve ·\r\nApp frameworks  LangChain · LangGraph · LlamaIndex ·                        BentoML\r\n                Haystack                                  Observability     Arize Phoenix · Langfuse · OpenTelemetry\r\nEvaluation      lm-evaluation-harness · Ragas · DeepEval  Security testing  garak · PyRIT · promptfoo\r\n\r\n   Takeaway — tools change fast; the categories don’t. Choose by category need first, then compare current tools.\r\nThe Complete AI Ecosystem · 40\r\n\f"}]},{"id":"ai-trust","title":"Trust, Security & Economics","pages":[{"number":41,"text":"The Complete AI Ecosystem\r\n\r\nSECTION 07 · LAYER 15\r\n\r\nTrust, Security & Economics\r\n\r\nThe controls that span every layer, and what the whole system costs.\r\n\r\nGovernance · Security and red teaming · Economics and sustainability\r\n\f"},{"number":42,"text":"§7 Trust · Layer 15\r\n\r\nGovernance is a control layer, not a final step\r\n\r\nEvery stage of the lifecycle carries its own controls.\r\n\r\nStage                    Controls\r\nData · 01–04             Privacy and consent, PII redaction, licensing, provenance, retention\r\nModels · 05, 06, 12      Fairness and bias tests, model documentation, license checks, model risk review\r\nApplications · 07, 13    Prompt-injection defenses, content safeguards, least privilege, human oversight\r\nInfrastructure · 08, 10  Identity and access, encryption, secrets management, vendor risk, energy use\r\nOperations · 09          Audit trails, AI incident response, monitoring, regulatory compliance\r\nPeople · 14              Transparency, explainability, accessibility, training for users\r\n\r\n   Takeaway — retrofitting governance costs far more than designing it in. Assign an owner for each control at each stage.\r\nThe Complete AI Ecosystem · 42\r\n\f"},{"number":43,"text":"§7 Trust · Deep dive 4.6\r\n\r\nAI security threats and defenses\r\n\r\nThreat              What happens                         Defense\r\nPrompt injection    Input overrides instructions         Separate instructions from data; least privilege\r\nIndirect injection  Hidden orders in retrieved content   Treat retrieved text as data; tool allow-lists\r\nJailbreaks          Users bypass safety policies         Safety training, classifiers, red teaming\r\nData leakage        Secrets or PII in outputs            Redaction, access-aware retrieval, scanning\r\nPoisoning           Tampered training or retrieval data  Provenance, validation, trusted sources\r\nModel extraction    Queries used to copy a model         Rate limits, abuse monitoring\r\nAgent misuse        Harmful tool calls, untrusted code   Permissions, approval, sandboxes\r\nSupply chain        Unsafe model files or packages       Trusted registries, safetensors, scanning\r\nDenial of service   Costly prompts exhaust capacity      Quotas, timeouts, input limits\r\n\r\nThe Complete AI Ecosystem · 43\r\n\f"},{"number":44,"text":"§7 Trust · Deep dive 4.7\r\n\r\nAI economics: cloud versus self-hosted cost\r\n\r\nDrivers: training · inference · tokens · GPU use · storage · network · energy · licenses\r\n\r\nUpfront      Cloud API                  Self-hosted or local\r\nPer use      None                       Hardware or reserved GPUs\r\nScaling      Price per token            Electricity and operations time\r\nUtilization  Instant and elastic        Limited by capacity you own\r\nLicensing    Pay only for what you use  Idle hardware still costs money\r\nBest when    Included in the price      Model license terms apply\r\n             Spiky volume, top quality  Steady high volume, privacy, offline\r\n\r\n   Takeaway — compare cost per successful task. A larger model that succeeds first time can beat a cheaper one that needs retries.\r\nThe Complete AI Ecosystem · 44\r\n\f"}]},{"id":"ai-reference","title":"Frontier & Reference","pages":[{"number":45,"text":"The Complete AI Ecosystem\r\n\r\nSECTION 08\r\n\r\nFrontier & Reference\r\n\r\nWhat is emerging, two end-to-end architectures, and a glossary.\r\n\r\nEmerging AI · Enterprise reference architecture · Local reference architecture · Glossary\r\n\f"},{"number":46,"text":"§8 Frontier · Deep dive 4.8\r\n\r\nEmerging and specialized AI, by maturity\r\n\r\nIN PRODUCTION             EARLY DEPLOYMENTS         RESEARCH\r\n\r\nEstablished               Emerging                  Speculative\r\n\r\nOn-device AI              Robotics and embodied AI  General world models\r\nTime-series intelligence  Scientific AI             Open-ended autonomous agents\r\nGraph learning            AI for drug discovery     General-purpose robots\r\nGeospatial AI             Privacy-preserving ML\r\nAI-assisted coding        Simulation for training\r\nFederated learning        Autonomy in set domains\r\n\r\n   Takeaway — judge new categories by evidence of deployment, not demos. Maturity labels shift every year.\r\nThe Complete AI Ecosystem · 46\r\n\f"},{"number":47,"text":"§8 Reference architecture A\r\n\r\nAn enterprise knowledge assistant, layer by layer\r\n\r\nLayer           Choice in this design\r\n01 Sources      Wikis, PDFs, support tickets, CRM records\r\n02–04 Prepare   Nightly sync plus CDC, parsing, chunking, PII redaction, permission tags\r\n03 Store        Object storage for files; hybrid vector and keyword index\r\n12 Models       Proprietary API for hard questions; small open model for routing\r\n07 Application  RAG with citations; ticket-creation tool behind human approval\r\n08 Serving      API gateway, streaming responses, caching, rate limits\r\n13 Connected    SSO identity, CRM and ticketing via connectors, audit log\r\n09 Operations   Traces, retrieval evals, feedback buttons, cost per answer\r\n15 Governance   Access-aware retrieval, injection defenses, retention, incident runbook\r\n\r\n   Takeaway — users only ever see answers drawn from documents they are already allowed to open.\r\nThe Complete AI Ecosystem · 47\r\n\f"},{"number":48,"text":"§8 Reference architecture B\r\n\r\nA private assistant that runs on one laptop\r\n\r\nHardware                     Runtime                Models         Knowledge                                             Interface\r\n\r\n32 GB Apple Silicon  → Ollama or LM Studio  → 7–14B chat model at  → Local vector store over → Desktop chat with\r\nMac or 12 GB+ GPU                                                  your folders                                          document upload\r\n                             local API              4-bit, local\r\n                                                    embeddings\r\n\r\nStays on the device                                       Decide deliberately\r\n\r\nPrompts, documents, embeddings, answers and chat history  Model downloads, app updates, telemetry, web search, cloud fallback\r\n\r\n   Takeaway — local trades peak quality for privacy and zero per-token cost; it suits drafting, search and summarizing.\r\nThe Complete AI Ecosystem · 48\r\n\f"},{"number":49,"text":"§8 Reference      Meaning                                         Term           Meaning\r\n                  A word piece; the unit models read and bill by  LoRA, QLoRA    Fine-tuning small adapter weights only\r\nGlossary          A vector capturing meaning, used for search     RAG            Retrieving documents to ground an answer\r\n                  How many tokens one request can hold            Reranker       Model that reorders search results by relevance\r\n  Term            Memory of processed tokens during generation    Hallucination  Fluent output that is not supported by facts\r\n  Token           Storing weights in fewer bits to save memory    Agent          A model that plans and acts with tools in a loop\r\n  Embedding       File format for quantized local models          MCP            Model Context Protocol, an open connector\r\n  Context window                                                                 standard\r\n  KV cache\r\n  Quantization\r\n  GGUF\r\n\r\nThe Complete AI Ecosystem · 49\r\n\f"},{"number":50,"text":"The Complete AI Ecosystem\r\n\r\nFive things to remember\r\n\r\n01 Data quality and permissions decide most outcomes.\r\n02 Start light: prompting, then RAG, then fine-tuning.\r\n03 Open-weight is a license; local is a location.\r\n04 Agents need narrow permissions, limits and verification.\r\n05 Governance and feedback run through every layer, all the time.\r\n\f"}]}]}];
